HWG_TOOL_DESCRIPTION = "Compile or run a .hwg flowchart file. A .hwg graph wires .hwo workflows with control flow and structured node I/O. Syntax: optional @graph [in(...), out(...)] declares graph inputs/outputs; (file.hwo)#node# [in(...), out(...)] declares a node bound to a .hwo file; append node policy as (file.hwo)#node# { retry: 2, timeout: \"10m\", cache: \"1h\", tools: [fs.read, fs.grep] }, where tools: narrows every agent in that node to those tools and is inherited by anything they spawn; !(file.hwo)#node# marks a manual node; #from# -> #to# is an edge; #from# => #to# is a fan-out edge (every => branch runs, and they must converge on a node declaring { join: \"all\" }, which runs once after the last branch arrives); edge metadata must be written between the arrow and target as #from# -> { on: verdict == PASS, maxLoops: 2 } #to#; never write metadata after the target node. @include \"path.hwg\" splices another file's declarations in place (path relative to the including file) so graphs can share node contracts; (schedule) { ... } sets schedule metadata; ``` ... ``` is a comment block. Node inputs must explicitly bind graph inputs or completed node outputs, such as in(summary = #research.summary). HWG sees only node-level .hwo out(...) values, never internal HWO agent locals. A valid graph needs one start path and at least one end node; bounded loop edges do not replace an exit path. action=compile validates without executing; action=run walks the graph and routes by the current node verdict or declared output condition. In laintas_cli, manual nodes create a paused run that can be resumed with /hwg resume <runId> [verdict] [outputs-json]."
HWG_SYNTAX = "HWG flowchart syntax:\n  @graph [in(topic: string), out(report = #publish.report)] optional graph contract\n  (file.hwo)#name#                                         node bound to a .hwo file\n  (file.hwo)#name# [in(x = $input.x), out(z: string)]       node I/O contract\n  (file.hwo)#name# { retry: 2, timeout: \"10m\", cache: \"1h\" } node policy\n  (file.hwo)#name# { tools: [fs.read, fs.grep] }            tool scope for this node\n  !(file.hwo)#name#                                        manual node; run pauses\n  #from# -> #to#                                           plain edge\n  #from# -> { on: verdict == PASS } #to#                   preferred output-field condition\n  #from# -> { on: PASS } #to#                              legacy verdict condition\n  #from# -> { on: verdict == FAIL, maxLoops: 2 } #to#      bounded loop edge\n  #from# -> { on: exists(dist/app.js) } #to#               file-fact condition\n  #from# -> { on: verdict == PASS and exists(out.md) } #to# composed condition\n  #plan# => #lint#   /   #plan# => #test#                  fan-out: every branch runs\n  (merge.hwo)#merge# { join: \"all\" }                       join: runs once, after all branches\n  @include \"lib/contracts.hwg\"                             splice shared declarations\n  (schedule) { start: \"09:00\", days: [\"Mon\"], tz: \"UTC\" }  schedule metadata\n  ``` ... ```                                              comment block\n\nVariables and scope:\n  $input.x          graph input, used in node in(...) or @graph out(...)\n  $loop.count       times this node has run on the current path; 1 on the first run\n  #node.output      declared node output from a node completed earlier on the current path\n  #node.output[-1]  previous output of the same node in a loop; first run may be empty\n\nRules:\n  1. Use ``` ... ``` for comments.\n  2. Every node binds a .hwo file.\n  3. HWG node outputs are only the .hwo file-level declared out(...) values.\n  4. HWG cannot read internal HWO agent-local outputs.\n  5. Node in(...) bindings should read graph inputs or completed prior node outputs.\n  6. Plain names in in(...), such as in(summary), only declare input names; they do not auto-bind prior node outputs.\n  7. Edge metadata belongs only between -> and the target node: #a# -> { maxLoops: 2 } #b#. Do not write #a# -> #b# { ... }.\n  8. A branch with multiple outgoing edges needs on: for every edge.\n  8b. If a node fails and the edge taken has no on:, the failure is unhandled and the whole run\n      ends failed, naming that node. Route failures explicitly if the graph is meant to survive\n      them.\n  8a. Conditions are atoms — PASS, field == \"x\", score >= 3, field in [A, B], exists(path) —\n      composed with and/or/not (or &&/||/!) and grouped with (). exists(path) is checked by the\n      runtime, not by a node, so use it whenever the branch really turns on whether a file was\n      produced; a node claiming PASS is not evidence the artifact exists. Paths may embed\n      $input.x and #node.field#; an unresolved reference makes exists() false.\n  9. Every cycle must include maxLoops on at least one loop edge.\n  9a. => is fan-out: every branch runs, so its edges need no on: and cannot carry maxLoops.\n      A node uses -> or => for all its outgoing edges, never both. Every => branch must reach\n      the same node declaring { join: \"all\" }; that node runs once, after the last branch\n      arrives, and reads each branch's outputs through #branch.field#.\n  10. A valid graph needs exactly one start node and at least one end node. A bounded loop still needs an exit path.\n  11. Node ids are unique.\n  11a. @include \"path.hwg\" splices that file's declarations in place, so several graphs can\n       share one set of node contracts instead of restating them. The path is relative to the\n       file containing the @include. A file is spliced once even if reached twice; a file that\n       includes itself is an error. An included file only needs to parse — it does not have to\n       be a runnable graph on its own.\n  12. Node policy is optional. retry is a non-negative integer; timeout and cache are durations such as \"30s\", \"10m\", or \"1h\".\n  12a. tools: [...] limits every agent in that node's .hwo file to those tools (exact names or\n       globs like fs.*). It only ever narrows — it cannot grant a tool the mode, role or\n       security policy already refuses — and it is inherited by any agent those agents spawn,\n       so a scoped node cannot delegate its way out. The completion protocol (task.complete,\n       agent_return) is always available, since a node that cannot report back is hung, not\n       contained. Use it to make a checking node structurally unable to write.\n  13. Manual nodes pause execution. In laintas_cli, resume the same run with /hwg resume <runId> [PASS|FAIL|verdict] [outputs-json].\n\nExample:\n  @graph [in(topic: string), out(report = #publish.report)]\n  (research.hwo)#research# [in(topic = $input.topic), out(summary: string)] { retry: 1, timeout: \"10m\", cache: \"1h\" }\n  (write.hwo)#write# [in(summary = #research.summary), out(report: file)]\n  (review.hwo)#review# [in(report = #write.report), out(verdict: enum(PASS, FAIL))]\n  (publish.hwo)#publish# [in(report = #write.report), out(report: file)]\n\n  #research# -> #write#\n  #write# -> #review#\n  #review# -> { on: verdict == PASS and exists(#write.report#) } #publish#\n  #review# -> { on: verdict == FAIL, maxLoops: 2 } #write#"
